00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:32.860
Builder, you're going to want
to subscribe to the playlist

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.520
so you don't miss out on
the more advanced episodes.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.470
And a quick aside before
we get started with GCB--

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.620
a container is just a way
to package and distribute

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.755
applications uniformly.

00:00:42.755 --> 00:00:44.880
It does this by logically
separating an application

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.550
process from the environment
it's running inside of.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.070
So inside this boundary,
parts of the file system,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.170
the networking stack, and
other running processes

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.195
are hidden away from
the outside world.

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:56.070
To make sure that the
application is portable

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.342
and behaves the same
way anywhere it's run,

00:00:58.342 --> 00:01:00.300
the application binary
is also bundled together

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.674
with its dependencies
and its environment.

00:01:02.674 --> 00:01:04.299
So this resulting
application container

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:06.247
is fast to start
up and shut down.

00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:08.580
It allows you to run your
application on any system that

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.746
can run containers, regardless
of the type of system

00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:11.780
it was built on.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.530
And it can be run next
to other containers

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.930
without knowing that
other applications are

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.472
running concurrently.

00:01:17.472 --> 00:01:19.680
So aside from being able to
build an application once

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.096
and just know it's
going to behave

00:01:21.096 --> 00:01:23.820
the exact same in
multiple environments,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.120
the container also simplifies
application development

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.870
because two container
images can do things

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.580
like both receive traffic
on their local host port

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.710
8080 no problem.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.560
So back to Container Builder.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.970
What happens when you want
to build a container image?

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.210
A lot.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.750
I like to simplify this process
by only using containers

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.910
to package and distribute
my application.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.530
I don't use them to
build applications.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.380
So keeping this in
mind, here are the steps

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.088
you need to run to
package and distribute

00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:53.490
your application using Docker.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.412
You need a few steps to
download and install Docker.

00:01:56.412 --> 00:01:57.870
Then you create a
Docker file which

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.764
has instructions on how to
build the container image.

00:02:00.764 --> 00:02:03.180
Then you need one or more steps
to build your application,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.388
and then a step to build
and package your application

00:02:05.388 --> 00:02:07.520
into a container, which
might download a base

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.960
image on your machine just
to upload the resulting

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.350
image into a registry where
other people can access it.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.944
Oh, and that didn't
even talk about running

00:02:15.944 --> 00:02:18.360
an instance of your container
or cleaning up the artifacts

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.950
that the build left behind.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.380
Wouldn't it be great if there
was a managed service that

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.060
could speed up building
and distributing

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.020
your images by skipping
your workstation

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.900
altogether and doing the
heavy lifting in the cloud?

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.267
Well, Google Container
Builder is that service.

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.600
You can use it with your
favorite continuous integration

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.490
tools or build
systems, because it

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.580
uses tools you know and love,
like Docker files and GitHub,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.010
so you don't have to learn
new tools to get started.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.490
And on top of that, it's fast.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.190
It has support for
custom build steps

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.770
which can be run concurrently.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.270
And there's support for using
build triggers so that you can

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.330
create images automatically.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.980
And all of this, all of this
comes with full integration

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.500
with Google Container Registry,
the same service that's

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.990
being used by Google Cloud
Functions, App Engine

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.560
Flex, and more.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.820
So let's see this in action.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.129
For example, if you
already have a Docker file,

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:06.920
you can just use it
like you normally would

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.570
to build a container image.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.850
Here you can see we have
a standard Docker file.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.933
We're going to build our
new application container

00:03:12.933 --> 00:03:15.992
from the Alpine 3.1 base image.

00:03:15.992 --> 00:03:18.200
Then we're going to add our
application binary, which

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.080
was built outside of Docker,
into our container's file

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:21.740
system.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.531
And then we're going
to run our application

00:03:23.531 --> 00:03:25.550
when the container starts up.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.320
Now I can use the gcloud
container build submit command

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.750
to submit that build
to Container Builder.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.460
From the output, you
can see that we just

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.310
built a container image without
it running on our laptop.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.680
So let's explain
what just happened.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.306
Since we already
had a Docker file,

00:03:45.306 --> 00:03:47.430
we used it with the gcloud
tool that comes standard

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.430
with the Google Cloud SDK
to build a Docker image

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.650
and submit it to Container
Registry, Google's

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.410
private container
registry, all in one go.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.760
Now, GCR works with
Docker images now,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.580
but other image formats
are coming in the future.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.474
It also lets you view
images by name or tag.

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:04.890
It allows you to create build
triggers that run and submit

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.620
new builds on pushes to code
in your source repository,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.080
based on either your
branch name or your tag.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.184
Once we ran that command, gcloud
packaged the source directory

00:04:13.184 --> 00:04:14.850
and built a container
image in the cloud

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.950
by executing the
following steps.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.200
We compressed the source
directory as a tarball.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.380
We uploaded the source package
to Google Cloud Storage,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.890
started a build request on
Google Cloud Container Builder,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.160
streamed the build logs back to
the user's console, tagged the

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.320
build images, and then
pushed those images

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.350
to the GCR I/O registry.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.850
And that's going to work for
any source repository directory

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.510
that has a Docker file.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:41.810
So instead of saying docker
build and docker push,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:46.330
well, you can just run gcloud
container build submit.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.210
So to recap, GCB is
a fast and easy way

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.399
to create container
images in the cloud using

00:04:51.399 --> 00:04:53.440
Google's infrastructure
that just plain works out

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.240
of the box with Docker files.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.210
And if you want to customize
your build process or even run

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.370
parts of it
concurrently, well, GCB

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.930
provides a clean and
modular way to do that,

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.540
which we'll be talking
about it in a future video.

